WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:06.030 position:59%
Everything is getting more expensive.
Between inflation, supply chain issues

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.790 position:59%
and the labor shortage, small business
owners are struggling.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:13.290 position:63%
So I'm here at the Texas State Fair in
Dallas to find out how one of its newest

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.510 line:92%
vendors is handling rising costs.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:24.440 position:89% line:92%
We’re going to finish the last 2 orders for the customer.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.310 position:70%
So we have carnitas,
barbacoa, carne asada, and chicken al pastor.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:34.210 position:95% line:92%
Add some melted asadero cheese and shredded barbacoa, onions,

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:37.840 position:97%
cilantro
and the barbacoa. Oh my God. I want to start by just going through

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.050
these receipts that you brought.
Tell me what we're looking at here.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:45.370
The first one that I’m going to
show you is the tortillas.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.750 position:75% line:92%
To have a taco place you need to have tortillas.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.520 position:98% line:92%
Yes of course. Otherwise there would be no menu. So I see that you've got $64 dollars

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.910 position:83% line:92%
total for yellow corn tortillas, which is 8 packages.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.100 line:92%
It went up like 0 a box.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.370 position:98% line:92%
So you're talking about 10% of what we used to pay before the pandemic.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:06.640 position:80%
So how much did that number used to be?
It used to be $48 dollars a case. $48 now it's $64.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.470
That alone is a big cost increase.
Yeah.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:12.170
Jesús isn't alone. In 2022 through
November,

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:17.630
food got over 10% more expensive,
with flour costs up by over 24%

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:21.490
and chicken up by 12%.
Supply chain issues, avian flu,

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.660 position:61%
droughts, geopolitical issues.
They all have an impact on the price of

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.430 position:59%
food.
And that leaves restaurant owners like

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:32.230 position:61%
Jesús making tough choices about how to
pass these costs on to customers.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:36.580 position:61%
I've tried to get the best product that
I can according to my prices.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.520 position:78% line:92%
When you pay $5 for a taco, people expect quality.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.940 position:59%
Is it five dollars for one taco?
I charge $5.75 for a taco, the octopus

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.270 line:92%
and people won't mind because first

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.030
the flavor, the
texture and the quality.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.600 position:61%
So how have you handled the increase in
costs for you?

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:57.520 position:84% line:92%
The traditional tacos, which is a barbacoa or chicken,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.220 position:86%
they're $2.99. Before the pandemic, they were $2.25.
The tortillas, the cheese, the meat and everything went

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.260 position:66%
up.
Even the produce, you have to increase it.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.750
People know those things and they
become more generous.

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:11.680 position:56%
They even tip more because they know
that the cost of living for

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.500
all my family, my employees,
I call them my family

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.190 position:63%
because we work 14 hours a day together.
So they have to pay their bills.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.920
So people that are very generous,
especially here in Texas.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.490 position:61%
How did you determine what your price
ceiling was going to be for your tacos?

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.940 position:97% line:92%
What you do in order to produce money and you don't overcharge

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:31.090 position:86% line:92%
is you get the food cost of how much you pay for a taco

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.100 position:70% line:92%
and then you do it one and a half times more.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.340 position:80% line:92%
For a lot of people, they don't increase the prices

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.650 line:92%
but they shrink the portions.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.270
But I wouldn't do that.
I won't do that.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.760 position:59%
I prefer to increase my price a little
bit

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:47.590 position:72%
than give a small portion.
That's really interesting because right now at

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.740 position:63%
the federal level, the view is that, OK,
companies will start to decrease their

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.080 position:58%
prices if we up interest rates.
But it sounds like you're saying that

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.990 position:70%
once you hit a certain price point,
you're not going to decrease the cost, right?

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.700 position:75% line:92%
No, I'm doing the same numbers that I used to do

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:00.580 line:92%
back before I opened,

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.150 position:61% line:92%
but now everything costs a little more.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.710 position:58%
Jesús learned the tricks
of the trade at home with his father.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.620 position:59%
I grew up on this business since I was
seven years old.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:13.540 position:78% line:92%
My father used to have restaurants in Mexico City,

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.020
so I tried to do more small things,
but this is what I love the most.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:20.050
Food is my passion.
Did you grow up in Mexico City?

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.670
I grew up until I was
I was 17,

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.340 position:73%
then I come here to Texas. I lived in New York,
Vegas, San Francisco

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:28.390
but the past 30 years
I lived here.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.620 position:97% line:92%
And your dad was in the restaurant space? Can you tell me more about that?

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.880
He was there.
He passed away many years ago.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:42.430
I'm pretty sure he
would be very proud

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:44.480 line:92%
to see what I accomplished.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:47.030
These tears are for happiness.
It sounds like a real family.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:52.570 position:77%
Yes. that's what I try to run, a family business.
And I told them, if I can do it then you

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.280
guys can do it.
It's not all about me.
It's about everybody.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.560 line:92%
It's very grateful for me to see

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.920
when they first start.
They come in now

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:02.800
they have better cars.
They have better houses.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:07.450 position:58%
And I know you've had an increase in
income recently because you're at the

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.520
Texas State Fair.
How excited are you about that?

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.630
Very exciting and very tiring.
Yeah, business is great down there.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:16.390 position:59%
So the fair came to you and asked you?
Yes.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.339 position:59%
Then invite me this year. There's 7
new vendors at the fair and by far I'm

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:25.900 position:58%
number one. Next, ladies.
Are you able to tell if you've made a

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.790 position:70%
profit from the work you're putting in at the
State Fair?
Yes.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:30.710
Yes, I've already known that I made
a very good profit.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:34.420 position:56%
From the number that I was thinking,
we increased it like 30$ more.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.900
I sell over 10,000 empanadas in
the past 20 days.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:44.590
Tons of tacos, tons of margaritas
and micheladas. It's the dream.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:45.660 line:92%
It's the American dream.

